The leading explanation is the **superposition hypothesis** (Elhage et al., *Toy Models of Superposition*, 2022). A **feature** is a property of the input the model tracks, such as 'this text is base64' or 'this token is inside a DNA string'. The hypothesis says:

- each feature is a **direction** in activation space, not a single neuron (activation space is the space of all possible activation vectors at a layer, with one axis per entry, and a direction is a unit-length vector in it);
- the model tracks **more features than it has dimensions**, so the directions cannot all be orthogonal (at right angles, with dot product zero);
- this works because most features are **sparse**: any given token activates only a few of them.

**Step 3, evaluate.** FVU and L0 come straight from held-out activations (254 sequences from Pile documents the SAE never saw). Downstream loss needs the model: a forward pre-hook on block 6 swaps the residual stream for the SAE's reconstruction, and the same hook can zero it or replace it with its training mean. Measured for the $k = 32$ run:

- clean cross-entropy 3.506 nats; with the SAE spliced in 3.713, a **delta LM loss of 0.208**;
- zero-ablated 13.22, mean-ablated 8.39, so loss recovered is 97.9% against zero and 95.8% against the mean (lesson 6);
- held-out FVU 0.1863, and 0.7% of latents never fired on the held-out set.

The dead-latent fixes were tested by changing one thing: with a random encoder instead of the transposed decoder, held-out FVU worsens to 0.2299 and 58% of latents never fire on held-out text (48% with AuxK on).
